From: rothman@netcom.com (David Rothman)
Newsgroups: eff.mail.cosndisc
Subject: [COSNDISC:120] "Children at Play on Information Highway"-W. Post Story
Date: 28 Nov 1993 16:09:47 -0500
Reply-To: rothman@netcom.com

"Caution: Children at Play on Information Highway: Access to Adult
Networks Holds Hazards" is a big, front-page story in this morning's
Washington Post. 

The story comes complete with a brief quote from Mary Ellen Verona, an
Ednet regular and perhaps a contributor here, too. In my opinion, she
has a common-sense attitude about these things. A small part of the Post
piece reads: "School programs are beginning to teach the
responsibilities that go with a computer password. Mary Ellen Verona,
who runs the computer science program at the Montgomery Blair High
School magnet program in Silver Spring, requires both students and their
parents to sign a form before the children type their first command. It
outlines the responsibilities, purposes and limits of school computer
use. Those who violate the school rules--say, by breaking into other
computer  systems--face a stiff penalty: They lose their online
privileges. 'For many of them, it's a tremendous punishment,' Verona
said."

A John Clement--presumably our own clement@kudzu.cnidr.org--makes a
cameo appearance just after the Verona quote: "John Clement, an official
at the National Science Foundation who deals with issues of
schoolchildren on the Internet, said once students get a sense of right
and wrong in cyberspace, 'We don't believe in prior control. We do
believe in nailing someone when they do something  wrong--and nailing
them bad.' Clement has made his teenage son pay off  his own
computer-related long-distance bill." I'd welcome John's own thoughts,
directly, without the Post between him and us.

The article, which begins with discussion of sex-oriented talk on
America Online and includes other nets, among them the Internet, asks
rather legitimate questions and should have been printed on Page 1. 

Just the same, I couldn't help but imagine some previously technophobic
15-year-old reading about sex-oriented MUDs in the Post, craftily
waiting a week or so, and saying, "Dad, I've changed my mind about a
computer for Christmas." Who says even family newspapers are free of
hazards? :-)

Seriously, now that educators are talking to the media about sex and the
Internet, maybe folks can be a little more outspoken about the need for
a TeleRead-style program. The same gigantic amount of newsprint devoted
to the hazards of cyberspace could also have been devoted to the risks
of *not* having worthwhile MUDs and comprehensive libraries online for
all schoolchildren, rich and poor. If newspapers like the Washington
Post are to run their own online services but retain journalistic
credibility, then they need to stop mechanically quoting the cant that
"The free market will take care of all"--and give more room to the other
side. 

Educators themselves must speak up for the public interest and hold Al
Gore true to his goal of schoolchildren dialing up the Library of
Congress (for full-text, let's hope, not just catalogue listings). 

In today's Post, the front page article mentioned his dream in this
context: "Vice President Gore's vision of a 'national information
highway' where every schoolchild can tap the resources of the Library of
Congress could be threatened by a darker vision." Presumably the
darkness should come instead from the hard-working owners of 900-style
phone services when they discover the Internet.

In the TeleRead proposal, I suggest that the program could include ways
for parents to limit the access of younger children. But nets themselves
shouldn't be censored, not even the "900" people. One other solution is
to put plenty of good, child-oriented books and software online for
students who need this material, supervise the children closely in the
beginning, and fortify them with the values they need to survive the
enticements of cyberspace. TeleRead calls not just for lower-cost
equipment and databases, but also for computers to be well integrated
into schools, and certainly part of this could include efforts to
educate children about good cybervalues. 

I recently testified on TeleRead before an interagency NII hearing and,
earlier, even managed to sneak the idea into the Post Education Review
(albeit not with the fanfare that John Schwartz's cybersex revelations
enjoyed). William F. Buckley Jr., hardly an enemy of Free Enterprise,
has endorsed the TeleRead idea, which would greatly expand opportunities
for the business community while also making e-books and ed software
universally affordable online. Ideally educators and the media--and
agencies like NSF--can catch up with WFB.

Usual reminder to innocent and not-so-innocent bystanders: the latest
teleread.txt (160K) is available by e-mailing me at rothman@netcom.com.

