I think Shaun's complaint against "network operators" is misplaced in this
instance.  Any information provider, be it newspaper television radio or
FreeNet, is obligated to follow the laws of the in which it operates.  By
cancelling the news group alt.fan.karla-homolka the National Capital
FreeNet (N.C.F.), acted in accordance with the publication ban.  Shaun
your beef is with the legal system rather than the N.C.F.. 

The distinction between a carrier of information and the content is
difficult when applied to Usenet and Internet in a Canadian context.  A
telephone company is minimally responsible for content that is broadcast
over their system, a television station or newspaper is entirely
responsible for the content they broadcast.  Internet and Usenet providers
fall somewhere in between, and the courts will decide where.  For Canadian
providers of information (i.e., Usenet and Internet) being treated like
the telephone company would be advantageous.  Because of the clear actions
of the crown (e.g., stopping distribution of newspapers at the border),
IMHO this is not a case that I would want to take to court and set
precedence with.  (Or bankrupt a FreeNet over.)

Shaun your posting is incorrect in two other ways which leads which leads
me to suspect that your comments are more posturing than substantive.  I'm
a N.C.F. user; I can get the information you seek three different ways and
I'm not an experienced user.  Also as I read the N.C.F. information panels and
NPTN information archives about FreeNets, the free in FreeNet concerns
giving the "community" access to information without economic limitations.
What you advocate ultimately would bankrupt the N.C.F., impose economic
barriers to access of local, community information and global information,
and prevent some and limit other's people ability to communicate with one
another.  So in this instance the N.C.F. cancellation
alt.fan.karla-homolka preserved the greater good at the cost of minor
inconvenience for a user (a gopher search with veronica; connecting to
another non-Canadian FreeNet). 

