Last night I made up a list of around 10 ways I could get a copy
of the Washington Post article electronically, even though I live
in Canada and am therefore expected to obey the court order. As it
happens, I got the article through Dialog. But this morning, in
the course of some routine work, I learned that I have one less way
of breaking the ban than I thought.

Infomart is a commercial news database service that's owned by Southam,
publisher of many Canadian newspapers. They have full text of all
Southam newspapers plus several others, and a variety of other Canadian
databases as well. Infomart also offers a gateway to Datatimes, an 
American service. This was one of my 10 ways. But this morning, when 
I logged in for something else entirely, I saw the following message:

                       WELCOME TO INFOMART ONLINE
  (for assistance call Client Services 416-442-2198 or 1-800-668-9215) 
   
     In order to honour the publication ban on coverage of the Karla
Homolka trial, Infomart will not be giving customers access to stories on
  this subject from U.S. sources through its gateway to DataTimes.  
          If you try to search or display these stories 
       you will be dropped from the gateway back to Infomart.

Isn't that nice? I love the idea that a database service my employer
pays a lot of money to is ready and able to track what I'm searching
for and decide whether I'm allowed to see it. Since I'm a librarian,
and getting information electronically is my job, this is pretty
annoying. 

Anyway, if a company the size of Southam doesn't want to even be
an accidental partner in ban violations, I sure as hell can't blame
the National Capital Freenet for not carrying this newsgroup.

Steve

