(This thread regading a court-ordered publication ban on the details
of testimony at the Karla Homolka trial for her involvement with her
husband in the death of two teenage girls. There has been much
hype over this in the last week as the police enforce the ban
by confiscating newspapers from people coming back from the states, etc.,
and on the usenet where systems are dropping newsgroups in case they
might be breaking the ban).

This paranoia is deplorable. The university is supposed to be the
bastion of free speech! I've seen papers (as we all have) from professors
that have shocked the nation - and I've seen articles by students that
embarass the highest levels of government to the point of scandal.

This is what free speech is all about. Now we have the usenet, and
this freedom is much easier to exercise. You'd think that the universities
would hold its principles dear, and defend it to the death, or at least
to the point of spending a few taxpayer-donated bux (which is a worst-
case scenario anyway).

(Are we going to get into the argument of "dollars for education or
freespeech? I'd rather be uneducated and live in a free society than
have an engineering degree in a place like the former soviet union.)

Instead, they're putting it to death themselves.

DESPITE THE FACT THAT THE BAN IS __ONLY ENFORCIBLE IN ONTARIO__, systems
in Quebec and BC have removed access to the group, even before 
some systems in ONTARIO did.

Didnt anyone care to actually read the ban? I've only recently become
reinvolved with this, so I havent had time to seek an official version.
However, I think the star is relatively reliable, and today's
states, on Page 26 ("unshackle the media in the homolka case" editorial),

*** "[the publication ban]...is only enforcible in ONTARIO..." (caps
mine)

Yes, we know, no one has the time to check and see if they actually have
to worry about anything. Which even we dont here - this post does NOT
break the ban - you are allowed to TALK about ANYTHING EXCEPT the
details of the TESTIMONY AT THE TRIAL - actually, we're allowed to talk
about that if we like, just the media (who?) isnt allowed to PUBLISH
it.

Yet, the slight wind of possible problems arises way out in BC and the
group is gone in a flash.

They have to meet a few criteria to actually break the ban:

A) they have to be in ontario (BC isnt)
B) they have to be the "media" and be "a publication" (who knows?)
C) the post must contain details of the testimony at the trial

Failing A and C clearly (except about 5 posts in ont.general so far, which
I assume we x-posted to a.f.kh), and not knowing about B, I'd say that
the importance of free speech CLEARLY and EASILY wins out over this,
even in this safe spot.

In ontario, we fail (A), obviously, but (B) and (C) are variables. Even
so, I still think that as long as there is no FACTUAL INFORMATION 
regarding the TESTIMONY, we are in a safe enough spot where free speech
should win out.

But its not my system. Its the universities. And what they say goes.
But I think that since they want to have this moral highground and have
their faculty publishing various papers on very touchy topics (Rushton, et
al.), then they should TAKE this position firmly, and defend all instances
of challenges to this right. I say they should do this even in the
case where A B and C fail, tho thats clearly ILLEGAL.

(UfT's main newsfeed's administrators mailed a letter to many admins
regarding this, and the feed of alt.fan.k-h has been removed. However,
many sub systems get their own NNTP feeds from various places, in
and outside Canada (tho the issue should be wether its in ONTARIO or
not)).

