It occurs to me that our Canadian friends may be missing a point in
all of this.

During most of our discussions, we lean (rather heavily) on the fact
that electronic media should be treated as any other media.  Our con-
versations are peppered with phrases such as "you wouldn't do that to
a newspaper" and "colleges should treat this as electronic student
publications, just like the student newspaper or journal."

If we believe that electronic media should enjoy the same rights as
its more traditional cousins, shouldn't we also be willing to accept
the same responsibilities?  

In Canada, it would seem that these responsibilities include the ac-
ceptance of gag orders such as the one issued in the Teale-Homolka
trial.  

I'll ask the same question about this that I (and many others) have
asked about other issues in the past, with one modification:

     If the other public forums can (*or cannot*) do XXX, why should elec-
     tronic media be treated differently?

I may not agree with the Canadian law on this issue, but I can certainly
accept the notion that electronic media should not be treated any dif-
ferently than traditional media.  I'm unsure of the exact provisions of 
the Canadian law, so I cannot offer any sort of compromise.

--Wes

