Toronto Star	(Ontario Edition)
Wednesday, December 1, 1993


Grisly details available on computer networks
by Michael Smith


Metro's two major universities are still carrying an international computer
bulletin board dealing with the controversial Karla Homolka case.

But at least two other Canadian universities, fearing contempt charges, have
blocked it.

Homolka was convicted of manslaughter in the slayings of Kristen French and
Leslie Mahaffy and sentenced to 12 years in jail.  A judge imposed a
publication ban on the evidence against her to ensure a fair trial for her
estranged husband and co-accused, Paul Bernardo.

Carleton University in Ottawa blocked the bulletin board last weekend,
following the lead of Montreal's Mcgill University, which blocked it a few
weeks ago.

But computer networks at the University of Toronto and York University are
still carrying the bulletin board -- one of hundreds of so-called
"newsgroups" carried on the Internet, a loose international assembly of
computer nets.

"We haven't even considered" dropping the newsgroup, said Warren Jackson,
who's in charge of U of T's computer network operations.

"Unless somebody really hits us over the head, we don't ban or censor
anything", Jackson said.

And Ian Taylor, the York vice-president in charge of computer nets, said:
"Historically, we have never monitored or censored any material coming from
the Internet and we aren't now".

Alan Greenberg, McGill's director of computer services, said he blocked the
newsgroup because "it appeared there was the intent to break the publication
ban" on the facts presented at Homolka's trial.

He said the university's lawyers -- after reviewing the court order banning
publication of all but a few details -- thought dropping the newsgroup was
"prudent".



