[Last Edit: 27-Nov-91 by Reg Quinton]

This is a policy statement adopted by the Computer Users Council (CUC) to
clarify the Usenet News Service provided by Computing and Communications
Services at the University of Western Ontario. The CUC can be reached by
E-mail as <cuc@uwo.ca>. The Usenet administrator can be reached by E-mail
as <usenet@uwo.ca>.

                          CUC USENET CARRYING POLICY

1. Why the University Should be Involved in Usenet
Usenet,  also  known  as  "the  news," is a whole new form of communication and
formation of  communities.    Traditionally,  universities  have  been  on  the
forefront  of  developments  in  these  areas.  It is therefore appropriate and
worthwhile for the University to be involved in Usenet.

2. Computer User Committee Serves as News Committee
The Computer Users Committee (CUC) will serve as the  committee  which  decides
which news groups CCS will and which it will not carry.

This committee will bear the full responsibility for the presence or absence of
any news group on the computer systems where managing the News  is  the  direct
responsibility of CCS.

3. CCS News Manager
A   CCS   employee,  the  News  Manager,  is  responsible  for  the  day-to-day
implementation of the decisions made by the CUC regarding the News, as  far  as
they  concern  systems  that  are  under management by CCS.  Specifically, News
Manager is responsible for the implementation  of  the  CUC's  Usenet  Carrying
Policy, on systems that are managed by CCS.

4. Carrying Policy
Sections  5  through  9  of  this document describes the CUC's carrying policy,
which states the general  principles  that  will  be  followed  in  making  the
decisions described in section 2.

5. General Equivalence of News Groups
Some  of the news groups bear a clear and direct relation to academic research,
for example sci.* (scientific topics)  and  comp.*  (computer-related  topics).
Although  for other groups this relation may not be so immediately obvious, the
principle holds that all human activity is a worthwhile  subject  for  academic
interest.

Furthermore,  as stated in section 1, it is worthwhile for the University to be
involved in all of Usenet.

Therefore, as a  general  rule,  all  groups  will  be  considered  as  equally
appropriate  for the University to carry.  Exceptions to this rule are given in
the following sections.

6. Policy Regarding Potentially Offensive News Groups
There is a wide variety in the things to which people take offense.   What  may
be  offensive  to  one  person  may  be completely innocuous to another.  It is
therefore not possible to make an exhaustive list of all that is offensive.

Furthermore, it makes no sense that something should not be  available  to  one
person  because  it  offends  someone  else.  In addition to this, a university
traditionally refrains from telling people what they can and cannot read.

It is however reasonable that an effort be made that no  one  be  inadvertently
exposed to something he or she considers offensive.

To gain access to a Usenet news group, one has to actively select it.  In doing
so, one always has to read the name of the group.  These names give  sufficient
warning  regarding  the  contents.    For  example,  the news group with sexual
contents is called alt.sex,  so  people  who  are  easily  offended  by  sexual
material  should have no difficulties in avoiding it.  The same goes for groups
like soc.culture.jewish and soc.culture.arabic,  where  the  contents  of  each
group may be (politically) offensive to members of the other group.

As  a  result,  there  will  in general be no need to take any action regarding
potentially offensive news groups.  Should it prove that a  group  consistently
contains offensive material while the name of the group does not indicate this,
then the committee will consider if any action should be taken  regarding  this
group.

7. Policy Regarding Harmful or Illegal News Groups
If  it  can be demonstrated beyond a reasonable doubt that a certain news group
consistently does direct and demonstrable harm to a person or persons, than  it
should  be considered if action should be taken regarding that group.  The same
applies to a news group whose contents are obviously illegal.

An offensive news group as described in section 6,  is  not  considered  to  be
harmful  in  the  sense  of section 7. An example of this kind of harm could be
incitement to violence against one or more identifiable persons.

8. Policy Regarding Offensive Articles Posted from UWO
If it comes to the attention of the News Manager that a  sufficient  number  of
people  consider  postings  made  by  someone from UWO offensive, then the News
Manager will bring this to the attention of the poster.  The News Manager  will
also  ask  the  poster  to  refrain  from  such  postings as a matter of common
courtesy.  If the poster does not comply, no  further  action  will  be  taken,
unless  the postings can reasonably be considered libellous.  In that case, the
postings will be considered harmful, as described in section 9.

9. Policy Regarding Harmful or Illegal Articles Posted from UWO
If it comes to the attention of the News Manager that someone  from  UWO  posts
articles  of  which  it can be demonstrated beyond a reasonable doubt that they
may do direct and demonstrable harm to a  person  or  persons,  then  the  News
Manager  will  order  the  poster to refrain from such postings.  If the poster
does not comply, the News Manager will refer the matter to the CUC.    The  CUC
will  review  the  matter,  and  may decide to deny the poster future access to
Usenet, if necessary by removing the poster's computer account(s).    The  same
applies to articles that are obviously illegal.

An  offensive posting as described in section 8 is not considered to be harmful
in the sense of section 9, unless the  posting  can  reasonably  be  considered
illegal.

10. Decision on Initial State
The   CUC  will  review  all  news  groups  that  exist  at  the  time  of  its
establishment, and decide which ones to carry and not to  carry,  basing  these
decisions on the policy described in section 4.

11. Decisions on New News Groups
The CCS News Manager will inform the CUC of the arrival of each new news group.
The CUC will then decide whether  or  not  to  carry  the  group,  basing  this
decision on the policy referred to in section 4.

11.1. Exemption from Informing CUC in Obvious Cases
The  CCS  News  Manager does not need to inform the CUC of the arrival of a new
news group, if it is clear a prima facie that carrying the group will not be in
conflict  with  the  CUC policy described in section 4.  In this case, the news
group will be carried without explicit approval by the CUC.


