=======================================================================

                The University of Western Ontario
       Department of Computing and Communications Services


                    Computer Users' Committee


                     Report on Usenet Policy

Background


The distributed conferencing system on the world-wide Internet
network (Usenet News), includes thousands of "newsgroups", or
"conferences", to which tens of thousands of "postings" are made
daily.  Many of these conferences are not moderated, which means
that, prior to their distribution around the world, the contents of
particular postings are not reviewed by anyone except the
individuals submitting them.

Along with every organization on the Internet, the University,
through the facilities operated by Computing and Communications
Services (CCS), has access to Usenet automatically, and hundreds of
members of the campus community avail themselves of this service. 
If all of these hundreds of users were to download daily to their
personal computers copies of all the new postings from some
"source", or "feed" elsewhere on the Internet, the total load on
the network would be intolerable.  As a result, CCS, along with
most other organizations on the Internet, downloads a copy of new
postings from the nearest remote feed, and provides campus users
access to Usenet from a "local" feed for a period of a few days
(currently seven).  In addition to providing this local feed, CCS
also provides access to the Usenet system on the shared computers
that it operates for users across the campus.  The University is,
therefore, involved in the storage and re-transmission of these
postings.

Concern has arisen at many universities that, among these thousands
of daily postings, there may be some that could be judged illegal
due to their possibly pornographic, racist or homophobic content. 
Suggestions have been made that, due to this possibility, the
University should attempt to filter out postings or newsgroups
based on their actual or presumed content.  Note that our
involvement in the storage and re-transmission of this material may
be construed as "distribution" or "publication" of illegal
material, which could be judged a criminal offence, although legal
opinions on this question appear to differ.

The Computer Users' Committee (CUC) has reviewed this general
issue, examined the responses of other universities, and considered
possible actions. In particular, the Committee has considered the
following specific "proposals"  and has reached the following
conclusions regarding them:

(i)  Proposal 1:  That a local Usenet feed not be created at the
University:

          Response: The Usenet system is far too valuable to
          justify a broad-based attempt to block access to it. 
          Moreover, not creating a local feed would not
          significantly impair anyone's access to the system, since
          individuals could simply revert to the remote feeds. 
          This would generate unacceptably high traffic on our
          external networks, thereby reducing their effectiveness
          for other purposes.   This course of action is,
          therefore, not acceptable.

(ii) Proposal 2:  That newsgroups that might contain possibly
     illegal material not be included in the University's local
     feed: 

          Response:  A partial filtering of postings to the system
          based on presumed content (eg. based on the title of the
          newsgroup to which a posting is made) would not
          significantly impair access, and might generate
          additional network traffic.  Moreover, there is the
          matter of the legitimacy of censorship (however
          ineffective) based on presumed or possible content rather
          than actual content.  The CUC judges that, in general,
          the campus community would be against such arbitrary
          censorship.   This course of action is, therefore, not
          acceptable either.

(iii)     Proposal 3:  That possibly illegal material be removed
          from the local feed once it has been judged to be such:

          Response:  Given the volume of daily postings to the
          entire News system, it is not feasible for anyone to read
          all postings before they are placed on the system. 
          However, should a specific posting be received by a user
          of the system and its content judged to be possibly
          illegal, the CUC agrees that it is reasonable to expect
          that there be a procedure for that individual to follow
          that could lead to the posting in question being removed
          from the University's local feed (even though that would
          not prevent users from gaining access through remote
          feeds).  Even if this is granted, however, questions
          remain, including: How do we determine whether a posting
          is or might be judged to be illegal?  and  How can timely
          decisions regarding this matter be made, given that all
          postings are automatically removed from the local feed
          after a few days in any event? 

Given this analysis of the options, the CUC is agreed that a
general procedure should be defined to allow people to register
complaints about the content of particular postings.  The two
questions that are at the heart of the matter, however, remain
largely unanswered in this proposed policy.  The question of
process, or how to decide whether a posting might be judged to be
illegal, can probably never be answered with a committee procedure,
since the definitive answer can only come from a court decision. 
Nevertheless, the Committee might reach a conclusion that action is
warranted even though a court decision has not been received. 
Accordingly, it has been left to the CUC to address the matter in
the context of particular complaints that it receives.  The
Committee will have recourse to legal advice. The question of what
the Committee will do once a decision has been made is also not
answered definitively, since the time for some actions,
specifically removing the posting, will often have passed before
the CUC is able to come to a decision.  Nevertheless, the option of
simply reducing the time that a posting is left on the system has
been identified as one possible course of action.  Another would be
for the CUC to reduce the time an entire newsgroup is left on the
system.  It might take this latter action for practical reasons in
any event (eg concern over the use of limited disk capacity), but
this decision might also be taken in the context of complaints
regarding particular newsgroups.  Finally, the option of assisting
the complainant to contact the individual who made the posting has
been identified, since it might wind up being the most effective,
and the most frequently enacted.

======================================================================


                        Proposed Usenet Policy

1.   The Computer Users' Committee (CUC) is responsible for
     reviewing and approving all Usenet-related policy proposals
     from Computing and Communications Services (CCS), including
     those relating to the newsgroups to be carried in the
     University's local feed.

2.   In its publications regarding Usenet, CCS should make it clear
     that the individual user bears the primary responsibility for
     the material that he or she chooses to send or display on the
     network or on the University's computer systems.

3.   Anyone who wishes to lodge a complaint regarding the content
     of a particular newsgroup posting should file a complaint with
     CUC in accord with the following procedure:

     (i)  A complainant should register his or her complaint with
          the News Manager in CCS.  The complaint should be in
          writing, preferably via e-mail, and should define the
          newsgroup and the posting of concern and specify how the
          material was obtained by the complainant.

     (ii) The News Manager will immediately forward this material
          to the CUC, through the Director of CCS, and the Chair of
          the CUC will call a meeting within two days of its
          receipt.  The CUC will solicit a legal opinion if
          necessary.  The Committee might take the following
          actions:  reducing the time that the posting of concern
          remains on the local feed;  reducing the time that the
          newsgroup in question remains on the local feed;  asking
          CCS to assist the complainant to contact the individual
          who posted the material in order to register a complaint. 
          Copies of the decision, including reasons, will be sent
          to the Director of CCS and to the complainant, and
          relevant action will be taken by CCS through the
          Director.

    (iii) Appeals of CUC decisions may be made to the
          Director of CCS in writing, who will forward it to
          the University Computer Council (UCC), along with
          copies of the original complaint, as well as the
          CUC decision.  UCC will review this material and
          make its decision on the appeal through a simple
          majority vote.  This decision will be forwarded to
          the Director of CCS and the appellant, and the
          original complainant if this is not the same
          individual.

4.   All administrators of CCS and other campus computing systems
     should continue to be responsible for following up on reported
     abuses of the information technology resources under their
     control by the faculty, staff and students of the University
     who are users of their system(s), including abuses of the
     Usenet system such as the posting of possibly offensive or
     illegal material.

5.   Anyone wishing to lodge a complaint regarding suspected abuse
     of the information technology resources of the University by
     a member of the University community, should contact the
     administrator responsible for the system on which the abuse
     took place or, if it is unclear or unknown who that person is,
     or if the abuse is of a more general nature, the Director of
     CCS.
                                                                







